   "source": [
    "# Quiz: Visualizing Distributions\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-10-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Histograms are overall more sophisticated types of chart than scatterplots</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>True</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Histograms are a more sophisticated chart than basic scatterplots.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>False</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Histograms are a more sophisticated chart than basic scatterplots, and better at showing true distributions.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-10-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>You can visualize distributions using this type of chart:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>scatterplot</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A scatterplot gives a quick look at how data is distributed.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>pie chart</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Pie charts show how parts relate to a whole. Scatterplots show distribution.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>column chart</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Column charts are for comparing values and showing trends. Scatterplots show distribution.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-10-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>This library is particularly useful for building histograms</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>TensorFlow</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Matplotlib is the library with very good ways to build histograms.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>PyTorch</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Matplotlib is the library with very good ways to build histograms.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Matplotlib</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Matplotlib offers very good ways to show distributions with histograms.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-10-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Histograms can be used to analyze this type of data:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>textual</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Text data works after a transform, but histograms work with numeric data by default.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>numeric</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Numeric data works by default, but text data works too after a transform.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>both of the above</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Histograms work with numeric data by default, and with text data after a transform.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-10-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>In a histogram, a 'bin' refers to:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a class of data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Bins come from dividing the data into smaller groups of values.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a grouping of data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A histogram divides the data into smaller groups called bins.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>throwaway data</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Bins do not throw data away. They divide the data into smaller groups.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"dsb-quiz-10-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>To build a smooth density plot, use this library:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Seaborn</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Seaborn makes density plots that are smoother than stepped histograms.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Matplotlib</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Matplotlib histograms look stepped. For a smooth density plot, use Seaborn.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>PyTorch</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. For a smooth density plot, use the Seaborn library.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
